~ stonebreak.CEARL — Stonebreak's VRAM program, written in CEARL
~ (Compute Efficient All-purpose Resource Language).
~
~ Compiled once at startup by CearlBootstrap. This file is the PLAN — the
~ resource policy driving the real allocators: chunk-region arena sizes,
~ growth and trim, the LOD region batcher's pools, and the persistent-mapped
~ staging ring. Device code (kernels) lives with its owning system instead:
~ the GPU culler's kernel is the engine's cearl/engine/culling.CEARL. One concern
~ per file.
~
~ Tuning without a rebuild: copy this file, edit it, and launch with
~   -Dstonebreak.cearl=/path/to/your.CEARL      (or =off for builtin defaults)
~ A plan that fails to compile logs the error and falls back to builtins —
~ it can never brick a launch.

~ The min-spec card this game is validated against. The budget is DERIVED
~ from the real machine, never hardcoded: a budget is a ceiling for
~ accountability, not a goal to fill — the posture everywhere below is
~ "allocate the least that loses nothing".
pin FLOOR: i64 = 6 GiB

plan stonebreak

    device
        ~ ~75% of the card belongs to the game; the rest is the OS,
        ~ compositor, and driver. 'vram' is detected dedicated VRAM in bytes
        ~ (0 on drivers that can't report it) — 'otherwise' falls back to the
        ~ min-spec assumption. On the 6 GiB floor this is a 4.5 GiB ceiling.
        budget (vram otherwise FLOOR) * 3 / 4
        headroom 10%       ~ keep a slice free: pressure hits 100% before the budget does
    end

    ~ The native chunk renderer's 8x8-column region arenas. Initial sizes are
    ~ the proven pre-CEARL constants; growth is tightened from the old
    ~ x1.75/25% (≈35% average slack over live bytes) to x1.5/12.5% (≈20%) —
    ~ the cost is roughly one extra grow per region lifetime. 'trim' is the
    ~ counterpart: an arena whose live bytes fall under 40% of capacity is
    ~ repacked into a right-sized buffer, so crossing one dense area no
    ~ longer ratchets the region to that high-water mark forever.
    ~ 'grow sparse' (Phase 3): arenas reserve virtual address space once and
    ~ commit physical pages only under actual meshes — growth stops being
    ~ allocate-a-second-buffer-and-copy (the transient ~2.5x region spike is
    ~ gone, offsets never move), and trim becomes a free page decommit.
    ~ Falls back to copy growth on drivers without ARB_sparse_buffer; kill
    ~ switch: -Dstonebreak.sparse=off (watch the console for
    ~ "[MmsChunkRegion] Sparse arenas ENABLED/unavailable").
    ~ Arena sizes were re-derived with the chunk footprint lab
    ~ (Testing/chunk-lab.sh, 2026-08-20) once the atlas moved to the pulled
    ~ 16-byte quad format: a dense 8x8 region's atlas is ~1.3 MiB of quads, so
    ~ it starts at 1280 KiB (two fewer copy-grows on cards without sparse
    ~ buffers); water and stamp geometry get their own, smaller pools below
    ~ instead of inheriting an atlas-sized first allocation they never fill.
    pool chunk_mesh
        category CHUNK_MESH
        budget 35%
        priority 100               ~ last to give ground under pressure
        grow sparse
        arena
            vertex 1280 KiB        ~ 81920 pulled quads (16 B) per 8x8 region
            index 48 KiB           ~ 24576 u16 indices (unused by pulled regions)
            growth 1.5             ~ grown arenas multiply capacity by this
            reserve 12.5%          ~ ...and keep this much slack over the need
            align 4                ~ element alignment keeps quad math tidy
            trim 40%               ~ shrink when live bytes fall under this share
        end
    end

    ~ Near-water regions: a 64-column region carries 0.3–1.3 MiB of 20-byte
    ~ water vertices (lab tiers 8/16), so the first allocation is a quarter of
    ~ the old shared size.
    pool chunk_water from chunk_mesh
        budget 2%
        priority 90
        arena
            vertex 320 KiB
            index 24 KiB
        end
    end

    ~ Stamp regions: SBO geometry that isn't a unit cube (snow layers, stairs,
    ~ slabs, crosses) — ~45 KiB per chunk in a tundra, so a dense region sits
    ~ around 3 MiB; start at 1 MiB and let growth/trim follow the biome.
    pool chunk_stamp from chunk_mesh
        budget 8%
        priority 95
        arena
            vertex 1 MiB
            index 64 KiB
        end
    end

    ~ LOD regions inherit chunk_mesh's growth/trim policy and category,
    ~ overriding only what differs — 16x16-column LOD regions start much
    ~ larger so the initial ring fill doesn't grow-and-compact repeatedly.
    ~ Trim matters most here: the LOD ring crosses the widest terrain variety.
    ~ Re-derived 2026-08-20 for the pulled LOD format (LODQUAD16, 16 bytes per
    ~ cell/skirt quad): a full 16x16-column LOD region holds ~350 KiB of quads,
    ~ so the old 5 MiB first allocation (sized for 40-byte vertices) would waste
    ~ ~4.5 MiB per region on copy-growth cards. Index bytes are unused by
    ~ pulled regions and kept only for the legacy/compact formats.
    pool lod_terrain from chunk_mesh
        budget 20%
        priority 60
        arena
            vertex 512 KiB
            index 48 KiB
        end
    end

    ~ LOD water sheets stay per-vertex (COMPACT20): ~170 KiB per region.
    pool lod_water from lod_terrain
        budget 2%
        arena
            vertex 320 KiB
            index 24 KiB
        end
    end

    ~ The persistent-mapped upload ring (GL 4.4 path). Its budget IS the ring
    ~ size — the one pool whose budget is enforced, not just accounted.
    pool staging
        category OTHER
        budget 8 MiB
        priority 10
        storage persistent
    end

    ~ Below the min-spec floor, shrink the LOD ring's claim further.
    when vram > 0 and vram < 6 GiB
        pool lod_terrain
            budget 10%
        end
    end

    ~ Under pressure, distant terrain is the first thing worth giving back.
    on pressure > 85%
        shed lod_water, lod_terrain
    end
end
